<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Samtskhe</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Meskheti</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Samtskhe is a historic upland region of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> on the upper Kura, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhaltsikhe">Akhaltsikhe</a> and including the cave monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardzia">Vardzia</a>. From the 13th century its Jaqeli atabegs ruled it as a largely independent principality; it then fell under Ottoman rule in the 16th and 17th centuries before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> took it in 1828 to 1829. It now forms part of the region of Samtskhe-Javakheti.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samtskhe.jpg" alt="Samtskhe" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giorgi Balakhadze · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Part_of_Historical_Samtskhe_in_modern_international_borders_of_Georgia.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
